{"context":"The official plugin eka-mcp (the registry's only entry, ADR-030) ships an MCP server and installable artifact families, but its capabilities are invisible in the CLI command tree: a user must know the plugin exists and run its executable directly. The CLI's command surface is static (the five intent groups of sto:help-ux); nothing registers plugin-provided commands.\n\nThe B1 requirement (req:eka-mcp-production) asks for deferred command registration: commands declared in an installed plugin's manifest appear in `eka --help` and dispatch to the plugin executable. The trust question is central: a plugin binary is third-party code executed by the CLI, so registration and dispatch need a trust boundary that never degrades the CLI and never runs an unverified binary.\n\nThe dynamic-grouping amendment was originally scoped as a plan:cli-experience content change, but plan:cli-experience is LOCKED immutable (planning-state immutable, container completed) — a plan content amendment is no longer possible. The dynamic GroupID is therefore implemented as a runtime mapping in the CLI registration path (the cobra GroupID mechanism from sto:help-ux, rendered by the shared renderCommandGroups), not as a plan content change.\n\nThe boundary vs ADR-022/ADR-023: ADR-022 defines the behavior layer (skills define how an agent behaves; MCP transports capabilities — Decision 9). ADR-023 defined the obsolete `eka install` command (superseded by ADR-030, removed at commit 6600f38). ADR-031 extends the command SURFACE only: registered plugin commands are execution-surface entries that dispatch to the plugin executable under the bounded env contract — they add no behavior-layer content, no new Runtime API, and no skill semantics. Command surface != behavior layer.","decision":"Option A: B1 deferred registration with the G-series trust boundary.\n\nRegistration is DEFERRED: at every root construction (per process, memoized), the CLI scans the plugin directory for installed OFFICIAL plugins (G1: plugin.OfficialRegistry only — third-party plugins never register), probes each manifest (bounded: pluginProbeTimeout \u003c= 2s, 1 MiB output cap, env whitelist), verifies the binary against the checksum sidecar recorded at install (G2 anti-TOCTOU: the SHA-256 is recomputed at EVERY dispatch and compared against the recorded checksum — a swapped binary refuses deterministically), registers commands only from the plugin-directory instance (G3: plugin dir over PATH — a PATH-only plugin is visibly refused, a PATH copy shadowing an installed plugin is ignored with a warning), and refuses collisions deterministically (G4: a command colliding with a built-in or another plugin's command is not registered; first-wins in sorted order). The manifest probe is cached per process with a TTL and invalidated on reinstall (the binary's file identity changes).\n\nEach registered command is a cobra command under the dynamic \"Plugins\" group (GroupID groupPlugins, added to the root only when plugin commands exist — a plugin-free CLI renders exactly the five static intent groups). Dispatch runs the plugin executable with the declared args + the user's args under the bounded env whitelist (PATH, HOME, EKA_PLUGIN_DIR, SystemRoot — never the CLI's secrets), inherits the CLI's streams, and propagates the plugin's exit code.\n\nFailure semantics never degrade the CLI: a broken, hung, unverifiable or colliding plugin is SKIPPED with a visible deterministic warning on stderr; every other command works unchanged.\n\nThe dynamic grouping amendment to plan:cli-experience is NOT applied as a plan content change (the plan is immutable); the dynamic GroupID is implemented as a runtime mapping in the CLI registration path instead.","consequences":"Plugin commands appear in `eka --help` and the landing under the dynamic Plugins group, grouped by the shared renderCommandGroups renderer (sto:help-ux).\n\nTrust boundary is fail-closed: no command runs unverified (G2), no third-party plugin registers (G1), no PATH-only plugin registers (G3), no collision silently wins (G4).\n\nThe CLI never breaks because of a plugin: every registration problem is a visible warning and a skip.\n\nPer-process memoization keeps repeated root constructions cheap (no re-probe within the TTL while the binary identity is unchanged).\n\nplan:cli-experience stays immutable; the dynamic grouping lives in the CLI registration path (runtime mapping), documented here instead of as a plan amendment.\n\nCommand surface != behavior layer: ADR-031 adds execution-surface entries only; ADR-022's behavior layer and the MCP boundary (Decision 9) are untouched.","alternativesConsidered":"1. Register commands at install time (write into the tree once) — rejected: the tree is rebuilt per process; install-time registration would not survive a fresh process and would couple install to the running CLI.\n\n2. Trust PATH-discovered plugins — rejected: a PATH binary was never verified through the install path; G3 refuses it deterministically.\n\n3. No hash verification at dispatch (verify only at install) — rejected: TOCTOU — a binary swapped after install would run unverified; G2 recomputes the hash at every dispatch.\n\n4. Apply the dynamic grouping as a plan:cli-experience amendment — rejected: the plan is immutable (planning-state immutable); the runtime mapping in the CLI registration path is the only sanctioned channel.\n\n5. Register third-party plugins with consent — rejected for B1: the two-tier trust model (ADR-028) governs install consent; command registration stays official-registry-only (G1)."}